er key for an arbitrary message (Section 5.4).\n    \n    Changes from -01\n    \n       Added Appendix \"Relation to the MDI model\", a mapping of the\n       discovery document onto [MDI], and a pointer to it in the\n       Introduction.  No requirement changed.\n    \n    Changes from -00\n    \n       Editorial: the text was shortened, and explanatory passages were\n       removed or moved to the Implementation experience appendix.\n       Implementation Status was updated.  One wording correction: the\n       requirement to ignore unrecognised members applies to readers; -00\n       said \"index\".\n    \n    Acknowledgements\n    \n       Thanks to the authors of [I-D.jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape], and\n       to Chenguang Du and Siyu Deng for the discussion of minimum discovery\n       information on the DAWN list.\n    \n    Author's Address\n    \n       Aleksandr Artamokhov\n       Independent\n       Email: alexar76@rambler.ru--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation-01 (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation-01)\nciteturn18search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week;  | Author  |  | Aleksandr Artamokhov alexar76@rambler.ru\nEmail authors (draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation@ietf.org)\n\n | Title  |  | Federation Between Capability Indexes: Crawl, Reciprocal Identification and Admission\nDocument  | Document type  |  | Active Internet-Draft (individual) This document is an Internet-Draft (I-D). Anyone may submit an I-D to the IETF. This I-D is not endorsed by the IETF and has no formal standing in the IETF standards process.\n | Author  |  | Aleksandr Artamokhov alexar76@rambler.ru\nEmail authors (draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation@ietf.org)\n | RFC stream  |  | (None)\n | Intended RFC status  |  | (None)\n | Other formats  |  | txt html xml bibtex bibxml\n\nInternet-Draft  | Index Federation  | October 2026\nArtamokhov  | Expires 3 May 2027  | [Page]\n\nWorkgroup:\n    Network Working Group\nInternet-Draft:\n    draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation-01\nPublished:\n    30 October 2026\nIntended Status:\n    Experimental\nExpires:\n    3 May 2027\nAuthor:\n    \n\nA. Artamokhov\n\nIndependent\n\n# Federation Between Capability Indexes: Crawl, Reciprocal Identification and Admission\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis document describes how independently operated capability indexes discover each other by following discovery documents, how a reading index identifies itself to the index it reads, and an admission model that distinguishes a peer that is known from a peer whose catalogue is indexed. It also describes how an index can preview a peer's catalogue before admitting it. Payment, identity, reputation and invocation are out of scope. The document is intended as input to the DAWN gap analysis on federation between directories.¶\n\n## Status of This Memo\n\nThis Internet-Draft is submitted in full conformance with the provisions of BCP 78 and BCP 79.¶\n\nInternet-Drafts are working documents of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF). Note that other groups may also distribute working documents as Internet-Drafts. The list of current Internet-Drafts is at https://datatracker.ietf.org/drafts/current/.¶\n\nInternet-Drafts are draft documents valid for a maximum of six months and may be updated, replaced, or obsoleted by other documents at any time. It is inappropriate to use Internet-Drafts as reference material or to cite them other than as \"work in progress.\"¶\n\nThis Internet-Draft will expire on 3 May 2027.¶\n\n## Implementation experience\n\n  * An index whose seed list contained its own URI recorded itself as a pending peer and contacted itself on every cycle. Its self check had covered only URIs taken from other indexes' `peers` members; Section 2 now applies the check to all three sources.¶\n\n  * A service operated by an unrelated party announced itself, failed the automatic checks because it published no catalogue, and remained visible to the operator as pending and indexed by nothing, as Section 5 intends. It was later admitted.¶\n\n## Changes from -00\n\nEditorial: the text was shortened, and explanatory passages were removed or moved to the appendix above. Implementation Status was updated. No normative requirement changed.¶\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nThanks to the authors of [I-D.jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape].¶\n\n## Author's Address\n\nAleksandr Artamokhov\n\nIndependent\n\nEmail: alexar76@rambler.ru--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-00 (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-00)\nciteturn18search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; Artamokhov  | Expires 3 May 2027  | [Page] ... Internet-Drafts are draft documents valid for a maximum of six months and may be updated, replaced, or obsoleted by other documents at any time. ...     \"AIMarket Protocol v2 test vectors and conformance runner\", 2026, <https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-protocol/tree/main/test-vectors>.\n\nInternet-Draft  | Marketplace Discovery  | October 2026\nArtamokhov  | Expires 3 May 2027  | [Page]\n\n# A Discovery Document and Signed Catalogue for Capability Marketplaces\n\n## Abstract\n\nAutonomous software agents obtain capabilities from services catalogued by independently operated indexes. For an agent, or another index, to use such a catalogue it must first learn a small set of public properties of the index: what it is called, which protocol versions it speaks, where its catalogue is, and which key signs that catalogue. This document specifies a JSON discovery document served at a well-known URI that carries exactly those properties, and the requirements a signed catalogue referenced from it has to meet so that a reader can verify it without trusting the transport or the operator. It specifies nothing about how indexes find one another, exchange catalogues, or decide whom to trust; those are the subject of a companion document.¶\n\n## Status of This Memo\n\nThis Internet-Draft is submitted in full conformance with the provisions of BCP 78 and BCP 79.cite3†¶ Internet-Drafts are working documents of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF). Note that other groups may also distribute working documents as Internet-Drafts. The list of current Internet-Drafts is at https://datatracker.ietf.org/drafts/current/.¶\n\nInternet-Drafts are draft documents valid for a maximum of six months and may be updated, replaced, or obsoleted by other documents at any time. It is inappropriate to use Internet-Drafts as reference material or to cite them other than as \"work in progress.\"¶\n\nThis Internet-Draft will expire on 3 May 2027.¶\n\n## Copyright Notice\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 IETF Trust and the persons identified as the document authors. All rights reserved.¶\n\nThis document is subject to BCP 78 and the IETF Trust's Legal Provisions Relating to IETF Documents (https://trustee.ietf.org/license-info) in effect on the date of publication of this document. Please review these documents carefully, as they describe your rights and restrictions with respect to this document. Code Components extracted from this document must include Revised BSD License text as described in Section 4.e of the Trust Legal Provisions and are provided without warranty as described in the Revised BSD License.¶\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nMachine-readable catalogues of invocable capabilities have appeared alongside protocols for machine payment [X402], agent-to-agent interaction [A2A] and tool registries [MCPREG]. Each catalogue is operated by one party. Before a client can use one, it needs to know a few things about the operator's index that are public by nature: a name, the protocol versions in use, the location of the catalogue, and the public key under which the catalogue is signed.¶\n\n[I-D.jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape] surveys the mechanisms through which such properties are published today and finds them fragmented: each system defines its own well-known resource, its own schema and, where a signature is present, its own unstated canonicalisation. This document specifies the smallest discovery document the authors found sufficient in operation, and it specifies the one thing most signed-catalogue systems leave implicit: the exact byte string under a catalogue signature.¶\n\nThe document is written for discussion in the DAWN working group. Its subject matter — the public properties of an AI resource that a client needs before interacting with it, and the integrity of that information — is within that group's charter. Catalogue exchange between indexes, admission of peers and trust are outside this document; they are specified in [FEDERATION], which is offered as input to the gap analysis in [I-D.jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape] rather than as chartered work.¶\n\n### 1.1. Scope and non-goals\n\nThis document specifies:¶\n\n  * a discovery document served at a well-known URI (Section 2);¶\n\n  * the requirements a signed catalogue referenced from that document has to meet (Section 3), including the obligation on any signature profile to state its signed byte string, and one such profile (Section 5);¶\n\n## 8. Implementation Status\n\nThis section records the state of implementation at the time of writing, per [RFC7942], and is to be removed before publication as an RFC.¶\n\nThe authors operate a reference implementation of the discovery document and the `ed25519-pipe-2` profile, distributed under Apache-2.0, deployed on six public indexes they operate or are affiliated with. The discovery document is currently served at `/.well-known/ai-market.json` with the member names of the authors' pre-standard wire format (`manifest_url` for `catalogue_url`, `tools` for `capabilities`); an implementation submitting this document plans to serve both names during a transition.¶\n\nThree further implementations of the profile exist, written from the specification text and the vectors without reading the reference source: a Go directory node, a Rust verifier for catalogues and announcements, and a JavaScript verifier for receipts. All three are by the specification's editor, so they demonstrate that the text is implementable and found four places where it was not (each now fixed), but they do not demonstrate interoperability between parties with no common operator.【Kandoi, \"A Survey of AI Agent Discovery Mechanisms\", Work in Progress, Internet-Draft, draft-jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape-00, 3 July 2026, <https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape-00>.\n    \n[MCPREG]\n    \"Model Context Protocol Registry\", 2026, <https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/>.\n    \n[RFC7942]\n    Sheffer, Y. and A. Farrel, \"Improving Awareness of Running Code: The Implementation Status Section\", BCP 205, RFC 7942, DOI 10.17487/RFC7942, July 2016, <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7942>.\n    \n[RFC8785]\n    Rundgren, A., Jordan, B., and S. Erdtman, \"JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS)\", RFC 8785, DOI 10.17487/RFC8785, June 2020, <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785>.\n    \n[RFC9727]\n    Smith, K., \"api-catalog: A Well-Known URI and Link Relation to Help Discovery of APIs\", RFC 9727, DOI 10.17487/RFC9727, June 2025, <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9727>.\n    \n[VECTORS]\n    \"AIMarket Protocol v2 test vectors and conformance runner\", 2026, <https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-protocol/tree/main/test-vectors>.\n    \n[X402]\n    \"x402 Payment Protocol Specification, version 2\", December 2025, <https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/blob/main/specs/x402-specification-v2.md>.\n\n## Mapping to the authors' current wire format\n\nFor readers of the authors' deployed protocol, the correspondence is:¶\n\nTable 2\nThis document  | Deployed format\n--- | ---\n`/.well-known/agent-marketplace`  | `/.well-known/ai-market.json`\n`catalogue_url`  | `manifest_url`\n`capabilities`  | `tools`\n`signature_profile`  | implied; always `ed25519-pipe-2`\n`peers` (array of URI)  | `peers` (array of object with `url`)\n\nThe canonical string, digest rule and verification rule are identical, so a signature valid under one is valid under the other.¶\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nEvery normative rule in Section 5 exists because its absence caused a concrete failure in a deployed system: a missing digest that let one implementation federate nothing, a vector set that signed too little, a bare-string signature that crashed three verifiers, and a permissive Ed25519 library that accepted a forgeable key. The authors thank the authors of [I-D.jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape] for a survey that made the shape of the gap visible.¶\n\n## Author's Address\n\nAleksandr Artamokhov\n\nIndependent\n\nEmail: alexar76@rambler.ru--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-01 (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-01)\nciteturn18search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week;     draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-01 ...     \"AIMarket Protocol v2 test vectors and conformance runner\", 2026, <https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-protocol/tree/main/test-vectors>.\n\n | Title  |  | A Discovery Document and Signed Catalogue for Capability Marketplaces\nDocument  | Document type  |  | Active Internet-Draft (individual) This document is an Internet-Draft (I-D). Anyone may submit an I-D to the IETF. This I-D is not endorsed by the IETF and has no formal standing in the IETF standards process.\n\nInternet-Draft  | Marketplace Discovery  | October 2026\nArtamokhov  | Expires 3 May 2027  | [Page]\n\nWorkgroup:\n    Network Working Group\nInternet-Draft:\n    draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-01\nPublished:\n    30 October 2026\nIntended Status:\n    Standards Track\nExpires:\n    3 May 2027\nAuthor:\n    \n\nA. Artamokhov\n\nIndependent\n\n# A Discovery Document and Signed Catalogue for Capability Marketplaces\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis document defines a JSON discovery document, served at a well-known URI, that carries the public properties a client needs before using a capability index: its name, the protocol versions it serves, the location of its catalogue, and the key that signs the catalogue. It also states the requirements for that signed catalogue and defines one signature profile. Discovery between indexes, catalogue exchange and trust are out of scope.¶\n\n## Status of This Memo\n\nThis Internet-Draft is submitted in full conformance with the provisions of BCP 78 and BCP 79.¶\n\nInternet-Drafts are working documents of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF). Note that other groups may also distribute working documents as Internet-Drafts. The list of current Internet-Drafts is at https://datatracker.ietf.org/drafts/current/.¶\n\nInternet-Drafts are draft documents valid for a maximum of six months and may be updated, replaced, or obsoleted by other documents at any time. It is inappropriate to use Internet-Drafts as reference material or to cite them other than as \"work in progress.\"¶\n\nThis Internet-Draft will expire on 3 May 2027.¶\n\n## Copyright Notice\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 IETF Trust and the persons identified as the document authors. It is to be removed before publication as an RFC.¶\n\n  * Reference implementation (Apache-2.0), deployed on six public indexes operated by the author or affiliated parties. Since 2026-10-30 they serve this document at `/.well-known/agent-marketplace` in addition to the earlier path `/.well-known/ai-market.json`, which uses the member names in Appendix \"Mapping to the deployed wire format\".¶\n\n  * Three further implementations by the same author, written from the text and test vectors without the reference source: a Go directory node, a Rust catalogue verifier, and a JavaScript verifier. Writing them found four ambiguities in the text, which were corrected.¶\n\n  * Two services operated by parties unrelated to the author publish catalogues that verify under `ed25519-pipe-2` (checked on 2026-10-30 with the conformance runner in [VECTORS]). Which software they run is not known.¶\n\nNo implementation by another party is known to have been written from this document.【--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProfile for Aleksandr Artamokhov (https://datatracker.ietf.org/person/alexar76%40rambler.ru)\nciteturn18search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;   * draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation\n\n# Aleksandr Artamokhov\n\n## RFCs (0)\n\nAleksandr has no RFCs.\n\n## Active Internet-Drafts (2)\n\n  * draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation\n  * draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery\n\n## Expired Internet-Drafts (0)\n\nAleksandr has no expired Internet-Drafts.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery-00 - A Discovery Document and Signed Catalogue for Capability Marketplaces (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery/00/)\nciteturn18search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week;     Artamokhov                 Expires 3 May 2027                   [Page 7]\n    Internet-Draft            Marketplace Discovery             October 2026 ...                   runner\", 2026, <https://github.com/alexar76/aimarket-\n       | /.well-known/agent-marketplace | /.well-known/ai-market.json      |\n       +--------------------------------+----------------------------------+\n       | catalogue_url                  | manifest_url                     |\n       +--------------------------------+----------------------------------+\n       | capabilities                   | tools                            |\n       +---------------------------